#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171716 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "496fa617-6592-57af-89ac-b77d68df0bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/id #uuid "fbe81109-2a56-5dd3-bab5-4ac5cc6ca860" :question/math-academy-id "e-12458" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z) =  x-y-z$ on the intersection of the plane $x+y+z=2$ and the cylinder $x^2+z^2=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) =   x-y-z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+z=2, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=x^2 + z^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ -1=\\lambda  \\\\ -1=\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =-1 \\\\ \\mu z=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu$ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction. So, from the third equation above, we must have $z=0.$\n\nNext, we substitute $z=0$  into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=2$ and $z= 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2 \\\\ 2+y+0 & =2 \\\\ 2+y & =2 \\\\ y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-2$ and $z=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2 \\\\ -2+y+0 & =2 \\\\ -2+y & =2 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(2, 0, 0), \\qquad (-2, 4, 0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(2, 0, 0 )$ | $(-2, 4, 0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $2$ | $-6$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2, 4, 0),$ and its minimum value is $-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171717 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172135 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "355c5dfd-d06f-56ef-9c38-8dccf702467e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/id #uuid "b8496dce-3f8b-5516-874d-17d82cfb41b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-12459" :question/problem "Suppose we want to find the minimum of the function $f(x,y,z)=2xz+y^2$ on the intersection of the surfaces $x-z=2$ and $x^2+y^2+2z^2=3$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}⟨2z,\\,,\\,2x⟩=\\lambda ⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,\\,⟩ \\\\ x-z=2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = 2xz+y^2\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-z=2, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2+2z^2=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2z,\\,2y,\\,2x⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,4z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts are $2y,\\:$ $-1,\\:$ and $4z.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "496fa617-6592-57af-89ac-b77d68df0bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9268e23-f546-54e0-b2db-acf77c82b969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169156} {:answer/id #uuid "f4714de0-2de3-50b5-8f8b-4c9317b0ac8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169157} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e5d0af-007d-5268-81bb-b5d25ee9af59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169158} {:answer/id #uuid "355cb361-2b35-5c63-afc5-b2b40c97f410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169159} {:answer/id #uuid "7d31f461-f500-5aca-80bc-de96d84f3b66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169160}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1228fa7c-87d5-5c53-baa3-b516657b8840" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169155}] :question/id #uuid "332ef138-daaa-584b-88e1-280c181c6e29" :question/math-academy-id "q-162669" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x, y, z) = 2x - 2y + z$ on the intersection of the plane $x - y + z = 1$ and the cylinder $x^{2} + y^{2} = 2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172135 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "355c5dfd-d06f-56ef-9c38-8dccf702467e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Lagrange System With Two Constraints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96774258-cf71-5407-a75f-7fe7ab66105f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2xyz$, $-2z^{2}$, and $3z^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169162} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b3e109-ea7c-51db-94a4-ec7e12e249ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2y$, $-4z$, and $3z^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169163} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf93119-a3ab-5446-9dfa-33eb266ee246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2yz$, $2x$, and $3z^{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169164} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff1e5f8-306a-57bf-bcf0-64869f367494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y^{2}z$, $-z^{2}$, and $3z^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169165} {:answer/id #uuid "51bb28f2-5559-5c56-b648-a6b6329f87ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2xyz$, $-4z$, and $9z^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169166}] :answer-field/id #uuid "58d10346-2f0e-5ef5-8bca-1a94b1b9fdae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169161}] :question/id #uuid "e7b3533a-918e-5b93-aa55-224813aed39b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162670" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the maximum of the function $f(x, y, z) = xy^{2}z$ on the intersection of the surfaces $x^{2} - 2z^{2} = 1$ and $y^{2} + 3z^{3} = 5$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $λ$ and $μ$ satisfy the following system:\n\n${⟨y^{2}z, A A, xy^{2}⟩ = λ⟨2x, 0, A A⟩ + μ⟨0, 2y, A A⟩; x^{2} - 2z^{2} = 1; y^{2} + 3z^{3} = 5$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?"}]}
